Task: Sample character and demonstration command (Phase 3)

What was done:
- A script generates the bundled character from fixed coordinates: a small
  figure with an outline, on a thirty-two pixel canvas. Identical on every
  machine, which is the property that lets it serve as a reference.
- The menu command asks the core for a dash and hands the result to the
  adapter. It contains no mathematics and no document manipulation; if either
  appears there, it belongs in one of the other two layers.
- The numbers that give the dash its character live in a preset file. Changing
  how the motion feels must not mean editing the engine.
- The entry point now makes the extension's own modules loadable, which the
  application does not do on its own.
- A render script runs the same path in batch mode and writes out the animation
  and its numbered frames.

Verified:
- The rendered frames were inspected directly: two held beats, then a large
  step, then progressively smaller ones as the motion slows, settling at the
  target and holding. That is the decelerating profile the preset asks for.
- One undo removes the entire result. This is now an automated check rather
  than a claim: the integration test applies the motion, presses undo once, and
  asserts the frame count, the layer count and the tag count are all back to
  where they started. Twenty-nine checks in total.

Finding worth keeping - the limit of driving the interface:
On this desktop the editor runs through the X compatibility layer and only
receives synthetic input while it truly holds keyboard focus. With focus
elsewhere, keystrokes vanish and pointer moves are ignored, which means a
synthetic click lands wherever the real pointer happens to rest - worse than
doing nothing. The interface driver is therefore for looking at the interface
when someone is already in front of it. Unattended visual checks go through the
batch render instead, which needs no window at all. A virtual display server
would lift the limit but is not installed and installing it needs a password.

Also caught here: a language runtime cache directory was about to be committed;
it is now ignored.
